  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected today and tonight with locally heavy rain possible and a \nfew strong storms.  \n\nA strong cold front across VA early this morning will shift south \nand move across central NC tonight. The airmass ahead of the front \nacross central NC this afternoon will be very moist with PWAT values \nof 1.75 to 2.0 inches which is around 125% of normal. The airmass \nwill also be weakly to moderately unstable with MLCAPE values of \naround 500 to 1500 J/Kg. The low and mid level flow will be weak \nresulting in weak shear and limited storm organization. This will \nalso support slow storm motion with messy storm structures and \ncolliding outflows. A marginal risk of severe storms has been \nhighlighted, mainly across the western Piedmont where downburst \nwinds are the greatest threat. A bigger risk may be the potential \nfor excessive rain and localized flooding this afternoon and early \nevening. CAMs suggest a minimum of convection during the morning \nhours with storm firing toward midday across the northern and \ncentral Coastal Plain, then building west across the U.S. 64 and I-\n40 regions during the early and mid afternoon with the strongest \nstorms shifting south during the late afternoon and early evening \nhours. The deep convection will wane by the mid evening hours with \nscattered showers transitioning to areas of rain overnight, focused \nacross the western Piedmont. For the 24 hour period ending at 12Z \nwednesday, the heaviest rain is apt to fall across the Coastal Plain \nand the eastern Piedmont with amounts of 1.0 to 1.5 inches and \ngeneral amounts of 0.5 to 1.0 inches elsewhere. \n\nHighs today will range from around 80, perhaps the upper 70s near \nthe VA border and the Triad, to the lower 80s in the Triangle the \nmid 80s across the south. Lows tonight will range near 60 along the \nVA border and Triad and the mid to upper 60s in the Sandhills and \nsouthern Coastal Plain. -Blaes\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Cold air damming will bring much below normal \ntemperatures during mid to late week. \n\nThe surface front will push to the S and SE of the forecast area \nlater today, setting the stage for development of a robust, \nclassical cold air damming event that will dominate our weather \nthrough the end of the week. As part of the rex block setting up \nover the Upper Midwest and Manitoba/Ontario later today, mid level \nenergy will undercut the blocking high, crossing IN and the Ohio \nValley Wed before deepening to a slow-moving closed low that will \nwobble over the Mid Atlantic region and Northeast into the weekend. \nThis pattern will keep the surface frontal zone pinned to our S and \nSE, with steady advection into central NC of cool and low-dewpoint \nsurface air from a strong and anchored surface high centered this \nmorning over W Quebec. This surface high will weaken as it slowly \nretrogrades to the WSW late Thu into Fri, drifting into the N Great \nLakes and S Ontario in response to what is expected to be strong \ncoastal cyclogenesis just off the Mid Atlantic and Northeast coast \nby Fri/Sat. But the circulation around this coastal low alone should \nhelp maintain the low level CAA and wedge regime into Fri, despite \nthe likelihood of further drying aloft and increased insolation \npotentially eroding much of the CAD stratus throughout the day. \n\nThe result will be multiple days of below normal high temps, \nincluding highs from the Triangle to the N and W in the 60s to \naround 70 each day Wed through Fri. Daytime temps will be further \nheld down by copious cloud cover and areas of overrunning rain, \ndriven in large part by moist isentropic upglide focused at 295K-\n305K from Wed morning through Thu night, along with a series of mid \nlevel perturbations spreading in from the west. In addition to the \ncool days, we're likely to see a few nighttime lows in the 40s near \nthe VA border Thu night through Sat night. Rain chances will dwindle \nThu night and remain low Fri through the weekend, with diminishing \nforcing for ascent and plummeting PW. But the potential for a baggy \nmid level trough or shear axis holding across the region along with \na persistent low level flow from the N or NNW around the coastal \nlow, potentially spreading cloud cover into the area especially in \nthe NE, should result in highs remaining at least a category below \nnormal Sat/Sun. Given how hot this September has been so far, having \nseveral days of below to much-below normal temps will be a welcome \nrespite for many.",
